# Rank 1104 / CVE-2026-25223: Fastify computed the request's content type two
# different ways for two different decisions, and the two computations
# disagreed on whether a tab character ends the media type (CWE-436).
#
# lib/content-type-parser.js's getParser matched a registered parser against
# a Content-Type header by prefix, and its boundary check accepted a
# following space, semicolon, OR tab (charCodeAt === 9) as the end of the
# media type. lib/validation.js's getEssenceMediaType, used to look up a
# per-content-type body schema, split the header on `/[ ;]/` only, with no
# tab. A header of `application/json\t<anything>` therefore matched the JSON
# parser (tab treated as a boundary) but missed the schema keyed under
# `application/json` (tab not treated as a boundary, so the essence type is
# the whole string including the appended garbage), so the body was parsed as
# JSON and handed to the route with no schema applied at all.
#
# Confirmed against the real request path, not just the two functions in
# isolation: an application/json route with a `body.content['application/json']`
# schema requiring one field and additionalProperties: false receives a POST
# with `Content-Type: application/json\ta` and a JSON object holding only a
# field the schema forbids; the vulnerable revision returns 200 with that
# field intact, and the fixed revision returns 415.
#
# Pool commit 32d7b6add39d is the real fix (COMMIT_OK, named by
# GHSA-jx2c-rxcm-jvmq). It adds lib/content-type.js, a single ContentType
# class both content-type-parser.js and handle-request.js now go through, and
# removes the byte-code boundary check from getParser's prefix match entirely
# in favor of an exact match against the class's normalized string or its
# type/subtype pair; validation.js is unchanged, but a header this class
# rejects, or normalizes to something that fails every parser lookup, now
# reaches FST_ERR_CTP_INVALID_MEDIA_TYPE in content-type-parser.js's run()
# before the request body is parsed at all, so the two decisions can no
# longer disagree. No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap. Nothing in the ontology carries CWE_436, and the
# nearest concept by mechanism, code.AmbiguousQueryParameterSeparator
# (CWE_444, a sibling weakness under the same interpretation-conflict
# family, a query parser that treats a semicolon as an alternate separator),
# is bound only to a Python-specific `qs.replace(';','&')` idiom and has
# nothing to do with a Content-Type header, so it does not and should not
# fire here.
#
# Stating this class generically needs a relation between two functions
# reading the same untrusted value with different delimiter rules, one of
# them gating a security decision -- comparing the character classes two
# different pieces of code treat as a boundary is not a taint or presence
# pattern over one function, and scanning the two files together gives
# fifteen identical findings at both revisions, all pre-existing HTML-escape
# noise unrelated to this weakness. No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1104: the parser's prefix match treats a tab as a boundary"
  lang javascript
  file lib/content-type-parser.js
  reject_review code.AmbiguousQueryParameterSeparator
  code
  ```
  ContentTypeParser.prototype.getParser = function (contentType) {
    let parser = this.customParsers.get(contentType)
    if (parser !== undefined) return parser
    parser = this.cache.get(contentType)
    if (parser !== undefined) return parser

    const caseInsensitiveContentType = contentType.toLowerCase()
    for (let i = 0; i !== this.parserList.length; ++i) {
      const parserListItem = this.parserList[i]
      if (
        caseInsensitiveContentType.slice(0, parserListItem.length) === parserListItem &&
        (
          caseInsensitiveContentType.length === parserListItem.length ||
          caseInsensitiveContentType.charCodeAt(parserListItem.length) === 59 /* `;` */ ||
          caseInsensitiveContentType.charCodeAt(parserListItem.length) === 32 /* ` ` */ ||
          caseInsensitiveContentType.charCodeAt(parserListItem.length) === 9  /* `\t` */
        )
      ) {
        parser = this.customParsers.get(parserListItem)
        this.cache.set(contentType, parser)
        return parser
      }
    }

    return this.customParsers.get('')
  }
  ```

test "rank1104: the schema lookup's split does not treat a tab as a boundary"
  lang javascript
  file lib/validation.js
  reject_review code.AmbiguousQueryParameterSeparator
  code
  ```
  function getEssenceMediaType (header) {
    if (!header) return ''
    return header.split(/[ ;]/, 1)[0].trim().toLowerCase()
  }

  function validate (context, request) {
    const contentType = getEssenceMediaType(request.headers['content-type'])
    const contentSchema = context[bodySchema][contentType]
    if (contentSchema !== undefined) {
      return contentSchema(request.body)
    }
    return true
  }
  ```

test "rank1104: a single normalizer decides the parser and rejects a bad header before either lookup runs"
  lang javascript
  file lib/handle-request.js
  reject_review code.AmbiguousQueryParameterSeparator
  code
  ```
  const ContentType = require('./content-type')
  const { FST_ERR_CTP_INVALID_MEDIA_TYPE } = require('./errors')

  function handleRequest (err, request, reply) {
    const ctHeader = request.headers['content-type']
    const contentType = new ContentType(ctHeader)
    if (contentType.isValid === false) {
      reply.status(415).send(new FST_ERR_CTP_INVALID_MEDIA_TYPE())
      return
    }
    request[kRouteContext].contentTypeParser.run(contentType.toString(), handler, request, reply)
  }
  ```
